<!--
    Framework compatibility matrix EXTENSION (version= is the mandatory XML schema metaversion,
    NOT the FCM level - the FCM level is a separate, here deliberately omitted, "level="
    attribute; its absence is what distinguishes this as an extension rather than a leveled
    framework matrix, per
    system/libvintf/VintfObject.cpp's hasFrameworkCompatibilityMatrixExtensions()/
    getAllFrameworkMatrixLevels(), which scans system_ext/etc/vintf/ and product/etc/vintf/ for
    ADDITIONAL type="framework" matrix files and merges them into the combined framework matrix
    used by assemble_vintf's checkUnusedHals check - completely separately from, and NOT the
    same mechanism as, device/vivo/1907N/compatibility_matrix.xml (type="device", installed via
    DEVICE_MATRIX_FILE/BoardConfig.mk, used for an entirely different check: what the device
    manifest requires FROM the framework, not what HALs the framework matrix must recognize as
    declared). Declares this device's own vendor.*/vendor.mediatek.*/vendor.vivo.*/vendor.nxp.*/
    vendor.trustonic.*/vendor.factory.* namespace HALs plus a handful of legitimate
    android.hardware.* extensions (configstore@1.1, radio@1.6 variants), all flagged by
    checkUnusedHals as "in the device manifest but not specified in framework compatibility
    matrix" once PRODUCT_ENFORCE_VINTF_MANIFEST was restored to true. See device git history for
    the full investigation (including two earlier, ultimately-incorrect attempts using
    DEVICE_MATRIX_FILE and a plain prebuilt_etc to vendor/etc/vintf/, neither of which this
    specific check even reads).
-->
